When an SVG text node has a text-transform, Ladybird shapes the
transformed text and keeps SVG DOM indices on the characters before it:
The two capitals that “ß” uppercases to are one addressable character.

Gecko does the same. SVGTextFrame shapes through the nsTextFrame text
run, which applies the transform, and maps back to content offsets
through gfxSkipChars, so Firefox 151 counts 7 characters for an
uppercased “straße!”. Blink’s LayoutSVGInlineText::TextDidChange() takes
TransformedText() and then normalizes its white space, the same order as
here; Chrome 158 crashed on the “ß” page. WebKit transforms the text in
RenderText too, but it counts the transformed characters instead, which
makes it 8 in WebKit 26.5.

All three draw the transformed text, as wide as literal capitals, in
<text>, <tspan>, and <textPath> (engine-compare, Chrome 158.0.8127.36,
Firefox 151.0, WebKit 26.5).
